# Workflow: knowledge promotion

The rule, from `[knowledge-architecture doc path]`:

> If future developers or future agents will benefit from a piece of information, commit it.
> If the information is only useful while solving the current task, place it in scratch.

`[scratch dir]` is disposable and excluded from version control except its own READMEs. Durable
knowledge must never be stranded there.

## Where knowledge goes

| Kind of knowledge                                                              | Home                              |
| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| How the software works: architecture, contracts, data model, behavior, decisions | `[permanent project knowledge dir]` |
| Design system and accessibility standard (if this project has a designer function) | `[permanent project knowledge dir]` |
| How to run/build/test/deploy: local loop, ops, runbook, manual checklists        | `[permanent project knowledge dir]` |
| Process knowledge: role prompts, standards, workflows, process lessons           | `[permanent process knowledge dir]` |
| Notes only useful for the current task                                           | `[scratch dir]/<role>/` (delete when done) |

## How to promote

1. When a discovery, decision, or convention would help the next contributor, write it into the
   appropriate permanent-knowledge file **during** the slice — not reconstructed later from
   scratch notes.
2. Cross-link: reference related docs/ADRs; process knowledge references project knowledge, never
   duplicates it.
3. Record significant decisions as ADRs in `[adr dir]`, with citations for any external/platform
   fact.
4. Clean the corresponding scratch when the slice closes.

## Anti-patterns

- Leaving authoritative knowledge only in scratch (it's invisible to the next contributor).
- Duplicating project knowledge into process knowledge, or vice versa (the two drift apart).
- Copying scratch into a permanent doc wholesale (write for the reader, not the session).
